#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# binomial

#% text
# `binomial(n, k)` gives the binomial coefficient "n choose k".

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For integers `0 <= k <= n`, `binomial(n, k)` is the number of ways to choose `k` things from `n`: `n!/(k!*(n - k)!)`.

#% item
# `k` must be an integer. For `k < 0` the value is 0; for `k > n >= 0` it is 0 too.

#% item
# `n` may be a negative integer or a fraction; then `binomial(n, k)` is `n*(n - 1)*...*(n - k + 1)/k!`.

#% item
# Symbolic and machine arguments stay unevaluated.

#% item
# `binomial` is listable: it applies to each element of a list.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
binomial(5, 2)
#% output
#= 10

#% text
# The number of five-card hands from a deck of 52:

#% cell
binomial(52, 5)
#% output
#= 2598960

#% cell
binomial(100, 50)
#% output
#= 100891344545564193334812497256

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Out of range values of `k`:

#% cell
binomial(10, 11)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
binomial(10, -1)
#% output
#= 0

#% text
# A negative and a fractional top:

#% cell
binomial(-3, 2)
#% output
#= 6

#% cell
binomial(1/2, 3)
#% output
#= 1/16

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
binomial([4, 5, 6], 2)
#% output
#= [6, 10, 15]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A row of Pascal's triangle:

#% cell
table(binomial(6, k), k, range(0, 6))
#% output
#= [1, 6, 15, 20, 15, 6, 1]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The coefficients of a binomial power:

#% cell
expand((1 + x)^5)
#% output
#= 1 + 5*x + 5*x^4 + 10*x^2 + 10*x^3 + x^5

#% text
# The sum of a row is a power of 2:

#% cell
sum(binomial(10, k), [k, 0, 10])
#% output
#= 1024

#% text
# Symmetry:

#% cell
binomial(20, 7) == binomial(20, 13)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Machine numbers and symbols are not evaluated:

#% cell
binomial(5.5, 2)
#% output
#= binomial(5.5, 2)

#% cell
binomial(n, 2)
#% output
#= binomial(n, 2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `factorial`, `gamma`, `beta`, `expand`
